@article{ WOS:001468866100001,
Author = {Correia, Joel E. and Piaguage Lucitante, Justino and Weiss, Luke and
   Narvaez, Nelly and Lucitante, Luciano and Biaguaje, Anibal and Piaguaje,
   Yolanda and Suale, Alicia and Payaguaje, Adriano and Piaguage, Esperanza
   and Simmons, Cynthia and Esbach, Michael},
Title = {Stimulating reciprocity: How human-plant relations support Indigenous
   cultural revitalization and stewardship in the Ecuadorian Amazon},
Journal = {PEOPLE AND NATURE},
Year = {2025},
Volume = {7},
Number = {5, SI},
Pages = {1151-1170},
Month = {MAY},
Abstract = {Human-plant relations shed light on forms of reciprocity in Indigenous
   territorial stewardship. This article shows how Cof \& aacute;n, Siona
   and Siekopai (also Secoya or Airo Pai in Peru) Indigenous Peoples in the
   western Amazon collect, cultivate and use yoco (Paullinia yoco) to
   promote communal conviviality, reclaim once-threatened cultural
   practices and advance new forms of collective stewardship to promote
   social-ecological well-being. Yoco is a caffeine-rich liana closely
   intertwined with the daily life and spiritual practices of many
   Indigenous Amazonian Peoples, particularly within the tri-border region
   of Colombia, Ecuador and Peru. We centre Indigenous storytelling as
   pedagogy and methodology, something common in the Amazon and relevant to
   yoco, as it is consumed socially and often while stories are shared.
   Through collaborative transdisciplinary research, we assess the forms of
   relationality and reciprocity yoco fosters in three ways. First, we
   discuss histories, uses and cultivation of yoco. Second, we consider the
   divergent pathways that communities have had with yoco, from loss to
   recuperation of human-plant relations across time. Third, we show
   examples of how differentiated use of yoco in Cof \& aacute;n, Siekopai
   and Siona communities supports cultural revitalization, territorial
   defense and stewardship initiatives evidenced by renewed efforts to
   enhance intergenerational transmission of local knowledge. Cof \&
   aacute;n, Siona and Siekopai stewardship of yoco is not merely
   ecological management of a plant but represents a dynamic interaction
   between cultural identity, spiritual practice and political resistance.
   As Siona, Siekopai and Cof \& aacute;n communities confront external
   pressures such as deforestation, extractive industries and
   socio-political marginalization, relationships with yoco facilitate
   pathways to sustain cultural and ecological relations in the face of
   profound change. Reclaiming and maintaining human-plant relations is a
   form of self-determination that can inform effective and ethical
   biocultural conservation. Through yoco, the Cof \& aacute;n, Siekopai
   and Siona peoples demonstrate that biocultural conservation helps
   maintain social-ecological well-being while underscoring the importance
   of territory. The future of conservation must embrace Indigenous
   stewardship, where reciprocity and care for both human and non-human
   worlds are central.Read the free Plain Language Summary for this article
   on the Journal blog.
   Las relaciones humano-planta ofrecen una perspectiva sobre las formas de
   reciprocidad en la gesti \& oacute;n territorial ind \& iacute;gena.
   Este art \& iacute;culo muestra c \& oacute;mo los Pueblos Ind \&
   iacute;genas Cof \& aacute;n, Siona y Siekopai (tambi \& eacute;n
   llamados Secoya o Airo Pai en Per \& uacute;) en la Amazon \& iacute;a
   occidental recolectan, cultivan y utilizan el yoco (Paullinia yoco) para
   promover la convivialidad comunal, recuperar pr \& aacute;cticas
   culturales amenazadas y fomentar nuevas formas de gesti \& oacute;n
   colectiva que impulsen el bienestar socioecol \& oacute;gico. El yoco es
   una liana rica en cafe \& iacute;na, estrechamente relacionada con la
   vida cotidiana y las pr \& aacute;cticas espirituales de muchos Pueblos
   ind \& iacute;genas amaz \& oacute;nicos, particularmente en la regi \&
   oacute;n trifronteriza entre Colombia, Ecuador y Per \& uacute;.
   Centramos la narraci \& oacute;n ind \& iacute;gena como pedagog \&
   iacute;a y metodolog \& iacute;a, algo com \& uacute;n en la Amazon \&
   iacute;a y relevante para el yoco, ya que se consume socialmente y a
   menudo mientras se comparten historias. A trav \& eacute;s de la
   investigaci \& oacute;n transdisciplinaria colaborativa, evaluamos las
   formas de relacionamiento y reciprocidad que fomenta el yoco de tres
   maneras. Primero, discutimos la historia, el uso y el cultivo del yoco.
   Segundo, consideramos los caminos divergentes que las comunidades han
   tenido con el yoco, desde la p \& eacute;rdida hasta la recuperaci \&
   oacute;n de las relaciones humano-planta a lo largo del tiempo. Tercero,
   mostramos ejemplos de c \& oacute;mo el uso diferenciado del yoco en las
   comunidades Cof \& aacute;n, Siekopai y Siona apoya la revitalizaci \&
   oacute;n cultural, la defensa territorial y las iniciativas de gesti \&
   oacute;n evidenciadas por los renovados esfuerzos para cultivar yoco
   para mejorar la transmisi \& oacute;n intergeneracional del conocimiento
   local. La gesti \& oacute;n del yoco por parte de los pueblos Cof \&
   aacute;n, Siona y Siekopai no es meramente un manejo ecol \& oacute;gico
   de una planta, sino que representa una interacci \& oacute;n din \&
   aacute;mica entre identidad cultural, pr \& aacute;ctica espiritual y
   resistencia pol \& iacute;tica. A medida que las comunidades Siona,
   Siekopai y Cof \& aacute;n enfrentan presiones externas como la
   deforestaci \& oacute;n, las industrias extractivas y la marginaci \&
   oacute;n sociopol \& iacute;tica, las relaciones con el yoco facilitan
   caminos para mantener relaciones culturales y ecol \& oacute;gicas
   frente a cambios profundos. Reivindicar y sostener las relaciones
   humano-planta es una forma de autodeterminaci \& oacute;n que puede
   informar pr \& aacute;cticas de conservaci \& oacute;n biocultural
   efectivas y \& eacute;ticas. A trav \& eacute;s del yoco, los pueblos
   Cof \& aacute;n, Siekopai y Siona demuestran que la conservaci \&
   oacute;n biocultural ayuda a preservar el bienestar socioecol \&
   oacute;gico y subraya la relevancia del territorio. El futuro de la
   conservaci \& oacute;n debe integrar la gesti \& oacute;n ind \&
   iacute;gena, donde la reciprocidad y el cuidado tanto del mundo humano
   como del no humano son centrales.},
